{
  "id": "GlassAtrium",
  "name": "Glass atrium (玻璃中庭温室)",
  "fps": 24,
  "defaults": {
    "shot": "Nitia"
  },
  "notes": ["Origin: the gallery floor directly below the painting's viewpoint; +Y runs across the gallery towards the far curtain wall, +X along it, Z up; metres.", "Gallery: a glass floor (top at z = 0) on jade-glass beams over the garden, south of y = 4.18 and on the west wing; the stairwell (x -5.0..-2.16, y -1.6..4.18) and the atrium north of the edge are open.  The edge is guarded by a frameless glass balustrade whose top edge carries short jade stanchions between two rails (0.91 and 1.04 m) -- the painting draws the stanchions only between the rails.", "Through the atrium the view falls on the paved north end of the garden (1 m flags: their joints are the regular lines behind the railing) and, through the lower curtain wall, on the pink haze outside.", "Stair (dog-leg, glass treads): 14 treads (0.12308 m risers, 0.433 m goings) along x = -4.62..-3.12 rise from the garden at y = 0.32 to a landing at z = -1.354, then 11 treads turn west up to the gallery floor at x = -9.383; solved from the painted nosings (16 px rms).", "Envelope: curtain walls on a 2 m module at x = -12.46 / 13.54 and y = -6 / 16, a glazed roof at z = 7.22; the conservatory crowns a 117 m tower: the frame's top row of image looks 6 degrees below the horizon, so the pink seen through the walls is the city haze far below, and the blue glass facade at the top left is a neighbouring skyscraper 50 m away (its edge solved from the painting).", "Garden specimens (\"Painted ...\"): each placed where its mass is in the painting -- the centre pixel of the mass back-projected through the solved camera at the height of its crown; the random understory fills the beds between them.", "Two trees in the north of the garden lift their crowns to the gallery level: the yellow-green canopies seen past the balustrade (positions back-projected from the painted canopies).", "Air: the sunlit air fills the conservatory 10 cm clear of the floor and roof -- a volume boundary must not coincide with another volume boundary (the glass posts end on those planes), or the volume stack at the camera breaks and every camera ray is absorbed."],
